<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>Vault Guard</displayName>
  <description>Policies for the Vault Guard password manager.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_VaultGuard">Vault Guard desktop (WPF) and Windows app (MAUI)</string>
      <string id="Cat_VaultGuard">Vault Guard</string>
      <string id="AutoLockMaxMinutes">Maximum time before Vault Guard locks when idle</string>
      <string id="AutoLockMaxMinutes_Help">Sets the longest time, in minutes, that Vault Guard may stay unlocked without keyboard or mouse activity.

If you enable this policy, users can still choose a shorter time (or "Same as Windows" if Windows locks sooner), but they can't choose a longer time or "Never". Settings shows that the limit comes from the organization.

If you disable or don't configure this policy, users choose any auto-lock time.

Tip: to make Vault Guard follow the Windows lock, also configure "Interactive logon: Machine inactivity limit" (Computer Configuration > Windows Settings > Security Settings > Local Policies > Security Options); Vault Guard's "Same as Windows" uses it.</string>
      <string id="ClipboardClearMaxSeconds">Maximum time a copied value stays on the clipboard</string>
      <string id="ClipboardClearMaxSeconds_Help">Sets the longest time, in seconds, before Vault Guard wipes a copied password or other value from the clipboard.

If you enable this policy, users can choose a shorter time but not a longer one or "Never".

If you disable or don't configure this policy, users choose any time.</string>
      <string id="LockOnWindowsLock">Always lock Vault Guard when Windows locks</string>
      <string id="LockOnWindowsLock_Help">If you enable this policy, Vault Guard always locks when Windows is locked, the PC sleeps or the user switches account, and users can't turn this off.

If you disable or don't configure this policy, users decide (it's on by default).</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="AutoLockMaxMinutes">
        <decimalTextBox refId="AutoLockMaxMinutesValue" defaultValue="15" spinStep="5">Minutes:</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
      <presentation id="ClipboardClearMaxSeconds">
        <decimalTextBox refId="ClipboardClearMaxSecondsValue" defaultValue="60" spinStep="10">Seconds:</decimalTextBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
